{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "08-recursion.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Recursion and fixed points",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Write a recursive function without naming it, using a fixed-point combinator, and see why call by value needs a different one.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A recursive definition, $\\mathsf{fact} = \\lambda n.\\, \\mathsf{if}\\ (n = 0)\\ 1\\ (n \\cdot \\mathsf{fact}\\ (n - 1))$, mentions itself, and λ-terms cannot. Abstract the self-reference instead: $F = \\lambda \\mathit{self}.\\, \\lambda n.\\, \\ldots\\ \\mathit{self}\\ (n-1)$. A factorial is a **fixed point** of $F$: a $g$ with $F\\ g = g$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] The Y combinator\n> $Y = \\lambda f.\\, (\\lambda x.\\, f\\ (x\\ x))\\ (\\lambda x.\\, f\\ (x\\ x))$ satisfies $Y\\ g = g\\ (Y\\ g)$ for every $g$: $Y\\ g$ is a fixed point of $g$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "normal 2: Y g",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Each unfolding hands $g$ another copy of $Y\\ g$, as many as it asks for.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Factorial",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$Y\\ F$ has no normal form (it unfolds for ever), so the definition is kept as written; applied to a number, $F$'s test stops the unfolding.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "pred := λn. fst (n (λp. pair (snd p) (succ (snd p))) (pair 0 0))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fact := Y (λself. λn. if (iszero n) 1 (mul n (self (pred n))))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fact 2",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fact 3",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "That took 1525 β-steps, and $\\mathsf{fact}\\ 4$ takes over ten thousand: numerals in unary, a predecessor that counts up from $0$ every time, and arguments copied unevaluated and computed again. The work shows the first steps and the last, and says how many it leaves out. This is why real languages build numbers in.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Under call by value",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Call by value evaluates $Y\\ g$'s argument $(\\lambda x.\\, g\\ (x\\ x))\\ (\\lambda x.\\, g\\ (x\\ x))$ before calling $g$, and that unfolds again first, for ever:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cbv 3: Y g",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] The Z combinator\n> $Z = \\lambda f.\\, (\\lambda x.\\, f\\ (\\lambda v.\\, x\\ x\\ v))\\ (\\lambda x.\\, f\\ (\\lambda v.\\, x\\ x\\ v))$ wraps the self-application in a λ (an η-expansion), so it is a value and waits until it is called.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Z := λf. (λx. f (λv. x x v)) (λx. f (λv. x x v))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cbv: Z g",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Turing completeness\n> With booleans, numerals, pairs and a fixed-point combinator, every computable function on the numbers can be written as a λ-term (Kleene; Turing showed λ-definable and Turing-computable coincide). Which terms have a normal form is then undecidable, which is why the engine reduces on a budget.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lean has no Y: every function must be shown to terminate, and a recursion on a smaller number does. Yet the factorial is still a fixed point of its defining step.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "def fact : Nat → Nat\n  | 0 => 1\n  | n + 1 => (n + 1) * fact n\n\n#eval fact 5\n\n/-- The step that defines the factorial, with the recursive call abstracted. -/\ndef F (self : Nat → Nat) : Nat → Nat\n  | 0 => 1\n  | n + 1 => (n + 1) * self n\n\ntheorem fact_fixed : ∀ n, F fact n = fact n := by\n  intro n; cases n <;> rfl",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem fact_pos : ∀ n, 0 < fact n := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "The factorial is never zero. Prove it by induction.",
      "leanSolution": "  intro n\n  induction n with\n  | zero => decide\n  | succ k ih => simp [fact, ih]",
      "hints": [
        "`induction n with`: `fact 0 = 1`, and `(k + 1) * fact k` is positive when `fact k` is.",
        "`simp [fact, ih]` knows a product of positives is positive."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "normal 1: Y g",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Take one step of $Y\\ g$ in normal order. What is the term?",
      "hints": [
        "Put $g$ for $f$ in $Y$'s body."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fact 1",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $\\mathsf{fact}\\ 1$.",
      "hints": [
        "$1 \\cdot \\mathsf{fact}\\ 0 = 1$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Recursion without names is a fixed point: $Y\\ g = g\\ (Y\\ g)$. Call by value needs $Z$, which delays the self-application. With fixed points the λ-calculus computes everything a computer can, and so whether a term stops is undecidable.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
